  "strategy": "### Approach and rationale:\n\nWe tackle the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem by first developing a geometric parent constructor to evolve function structure across grid scales. Unlike previous optimization-based methods, this approach:  \n1. Maintains a population of correlated step-function templates in multiple grid sizes (64, 128, 256, 512)  \n2. Uses convolution filtering to enforce integral constraints  \n3. Implements gradient projection for local optimization  \n4. Dynamically balances exploration via controlled random mutations  \n\nThe key innovation is the geometric parent model that inherits correlation patterns while adapting to grid structure. The core constraint-handling ensures both sum=0.5 and box constraints through projection. We carefully time-distribute computations: 20% to initialization, 50% to optimization, 30% to refinement.  \n\n### Mathematical specification:\n\nWe minimize **C5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx** with constraints:  \n- Piecewise constant functions on {0,1,...,n-1} grid points  \n- \u2211h\u1d62 = n/2  \n- 0 \u2264 h\u1d62 \u2264 1  \n\nRepresentations:\n- 3D Fourier transform: \u210b(k) = \u03a3 h[n] e^{2\u03c0ikn/L}  \n- Convolution: ((h * \u210b)(\u03ba) = max correlation at lag \u03ba  \n- Projection: P(h) = argmin ||h-h\u2098||\u2082 s.t. \u03a3h\u1d62= n/2  \n\nOptimization formulation:  \nmin h\u2098\u2096\u00b2(h) s.t.  \n(\u2207C5) \u21bd \u211d(h) \u2264 0  \nh \u2208 {0,\u00b9}\u207f  \n\n### Implementation procedure:\n\n1. **Grid construction**  \n   - Initialize parents for each grid size using Thue-Morse sequence  \n   - Apply linear interpolation for grid transposition  \n   - Use projection to enforce \u03a3h\u1d62 = n/2  \n\n2. **Optimization loop**  \n   - **Steepest descent** with line search:  \n     \u03b8\u208d\u2096\u208a\u2081\u208e = \u03b8\u2096 - \u03b1\u2207\u2093J(\u03b8\u2096)  \n   - **Gradient calculation**:  \n     \u2207J = \u03a3 [h[n] sign((h[h]-h[j]) \u2207]  \n   - **Box projection**:  \n     y_i = clip(x_i, 0, 1)  \n\n3. **Time allocation**  \n   - Primary grid: 64 base points (20% budget)  \n   - Secondary grid: 128 points (50% budget)  \n   - Tertiary grid: 256 points (20% budget)  \n   - Final grid: 512 points (10% budget)  \n\n4. **Convergence criteria**  \n   - Normalize J(h) = J(h_max) + (J(final) - J(h_max))/J(worst)  \n   - Stop if improvement < 0.0001 or time expired  \n\n### Search choices:\n\n- **Gradient tolerance**: 0.0001  \n- **Max iterations**: 2\u00d710\u2076 per grid  \n- **Temperature**: 1.0 \u00d7 (2/log(t))\u00b2  \n- **Mutation rate**: 0.1 \u00d7 (grid size)  \n\n### Numerical robustness:\n\n- Maintain working memory for each grid (64\u00d7512\u00d7FLOAT \u2192 ~16MB)  \n- Use conjugate gradient for ill-conditioned Hessian  \n- Project \u2207J to maintain feasible descent  \n\n**Fallback structure**:  \nWhen projection fails, use geometric restart:  \n   h_restart = normalize(Thue-Morse(n))  \n\n### Runtime safeguards:\n\n- Grid-specific time limits  \n- Null-space detection in gradient calculations  \n- Dynamic learning rate: \u03b1 = 0.01 \u00d7 (2 / sqrt(t))",
